# Sunfront Sentinel Foundry — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Sunfront Sentinel Foundry, a wide, open casting works
where a gantry-slung crucible tips and pours glowing molten metal while great bellows
breathe beside it — built with `voxel-anim` by painting discrete opaque cells into a
rigged 3D model a game poses at runtime. There is no target model to copy: it must read
unmistakably as this working casting foundry and satisfy the animation contract below.

This is a **casting** foundry — it melts and pours metal; it does not hammer or stamp.
Build it low and broad, an open-fronted hall, not a tall tower.

This brief fixes what the Foundry is and how it must move. It does not give you a parts
list, joint placements, or pivots — working out the pieces a pouring, breathing foundry
needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the challenge. Invent the rig.

## How the tool works

`voxel-anim` places discrete opaque cells, one recorded operation at a time. Set and
clear single voxels, fill and stroke boxes, draw 3D lines and spheres, and mirror across
a plane; each op takes an opaque `#rrggbb` color, and there is no transparency. Global
`--part <name>` selects the part an op paints, and each part is its own volume, previewed
on its own; create a part with `define-part` before you paint into it.

A call **records only** and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw
`parts/<part>.png` and the assembled `scene/*.png`, then read them between calls.
`voxel-anim --help` is the contract.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It starts
  empty; cell coordinates are grid positions in this volume.
- x runs across the hall, 0–{{voxel.maxX}}. y runs up, 0 (the ground) to {{voxel.maxY}} (top). z runs
  front-to-back, 0–{{voxel.maxZ}}. **Forward is +z:** the open pour-face of the hall — where the
  crucible tips and the molten stream falls — faces toward z = {{voxel.maxZ}}. Up is +y.
- Build the hall symmetric about the lengthwise vertical centerplane where the form
  allows, with the casting bed centered under the crucible (the flank bellows may break
  that symmetry).
- The foundry is deliberately **wide and low** — a broad, open-fronted masonry casting
  hall rooted to the ground, filling most of the width and depth but only rising partway
  up the volume. It is emphatically **not** a tall tower with a central throat.
- Each part is sculpted in these shared coordinates, positioned where it sits on the
  assembled hall.

## What the Foundry is (and what is yours to invent)

The foundry must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A wide, low, open-fronted masonry casting hall — the fixed body of the works, rooted to
  the ground and filling most of the width and depth, its front opened so the pour reads.
  Build it in the brass and bronze plating (bronze on its underside and in the shadowed
  seams, sandstone panels for lighter structure), not a plain box.
- A raised **gantry** spanning the hall that carries a big iron **crucible** — a heavy
  vessel brimming with glowing molten metal, hung on trunnions so it can tip forward and
  pour.
- A **casting bed / mold trough** set below the crucible's lip, where the poured molten
  metal lands and pools — glowing in the solar-amber and solar-hot accents.
- A pair of great **bellows** on the flank — an accordion-ribbed wedge that squeezes shut
  and draws open to blast the melt.
- A clear solar-amber accent: the molten metal itself — a glowing crucible brim, the
  pour stream, and the pooled trough — so the accent reads from many angles.

Everything else is yours to invent — the silhouette and proportions, how the hall is
massed and roofed, how the gantry is trussed, how the crucible, casting bed, and bellows
are shaped, and how you break the foundry into rig parts and place its joints. Nothing
here prescribes a shape or a pivot; the brief rewards a bold, characterful design that is
unmistakably a casting foundry and animates convincingly. Leave the moving pieces
something to seat against — trunnions for the crucible to tip on, a bed beneath its lip,
and a clear mouth for the bellows — so they meet the hall with no gap.

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Masonry — primary plating (brass) | `#c69a4b` |
| Masonry — dark plating, underside, shadow (bronze) | `#7a5527` |
| Secondary panels / lighter structure (sandstone) | `#d9c48c` |
| Crucible, gantry, bellows frame, joints, mechanisms (iron) | `#565c64` |
| Deep recesses, shadow (dark iron) | `#31353b` |
| Molten metal — energy accent (solar amber) | `#ff9d2e` |
| Molten metal — hottest glow (solar hot) | `#ffd76b` |

The solar-amber accent is the team-tint region: give the foundry a clear, glowing molten
accent — a brimming crucible, the pour stream, and the pooled casting bed, with a
solar-hot highlight at the hottest metal — so the accent reads from multiple angles.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Both are self-playing and looping, so the foundry runs continuously on its own
with no caller, and the hall itself stays fixed throughout. Author each with `voxel-anim
define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the period and interpolation so the motion
carries weight rather than sliding linearly.

- **`crucible_pour`** — the crucible tips forward on its trunnions, pours a stream of
  molten metal down into the casting bed, holds at the bottom of the tip, and rights
  back level each loop, moving with the weight of a full vessel. The crucible moves; the
  hall, gantry, and bellows hold.
- **`bellows_breathe`** — the great bellows squeeze shut and draw back open in a slow,
  weighted breath each loop. The bellows move; the hall and crucible hold.

You may add extra parts, joints, and self-playing animations of your own (a flickering
glow, a secondary counterweight, a drifting heat-haze); you must produce these two
animations, by these names, and must not contradict them — the hall itself never moves,
only the crucible and the bellows, and nothing hammers or stamps.

## Working the tool

Define your parts with `define-part`, paint each with `--part <name>`, set pivots
with `set-pivot`, place joints with `define-joint`, and author the two animations'
keyframes — running `voxel-anim render` and reading `parts/<part>.png` and the
`scene/*.png` previews between calls to confirm the parts fit, the crucible hangs over
the casting bed with room to tip and pour, the bellows seat on the flank with room to
squeeze, and the animations read with weight. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish
so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part
scores as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim
render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check
the motion). The recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are the authoritative output. Run
`voxel-anim --help` for the available operations, the rig subcommands, and the animation
subcommands, and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for each one's exact flags.
